WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.886
Agent telemetry starts with sessions and
events.

2
00:00:04.966 --> 00:00:11.442
A session may include user requests, model
calls, retrieval operations,

3
00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:17.997
tool selections, tool results, memory
reads, memory writes, approvals,

4
00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:24.657
policy decisions, errors, latency, and
cost or token usage at a high level.

5
00:00:24.737 --> 00:00:32.152
Each event should explain what happened
and how it relates to the wider interaction.

6
00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:38.603
Isolated log lines are much less useful
than a trace that connects cause,

7
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:40.772
decision, and outcome.

8
00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:43.671
Correlation IDs are essential.

9
00:00:43.751 --> 00:00:50.331
A single user request may pass through a
frontend, backend, model gateway,

10
00:00:50.411 --> 00:00:57.304
retrieval service, tool service, approval
workflow, and downstream system.

11
00:00:57.384 --> 00:01:05.113
If each system uses unrelated identifiers,
investigation becomes slow and uncertain.

12
00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:12.399
A shared trace or correlation identifier
lets teams reconstruct the path from the

13
00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:16.657
original request to the final answer or
action,

14
00:01:16.737 --> 00:01:21.228
even when events are distributed across
services.

15
00:01:21.308 --> 00:01:28.305
Telemetry should include model and prompt
metadata without blindly recording

16
00:01:28.385 --> 00:01:30.787
sensitive prompt content.

17
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:36.611
Teams often need model version, prompt
version, policy version,

18
00:01:36.691 --> 00:01:42.540
tool version, retrieval source reference,
and decision outcome.

19
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:49.931
They may not need full raw prompts, full
documents, credentials, or personal data.

20
00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:57.217
Good event design separates metadata
needed for investigation from content that

21
00:01:57.297 --> 00:02:02.310
would create unnecessary privacy or
security exposure.

22
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:09.597
The logging architecture should tell the
story of what happened without exposing

23
00:02:09.677 --> 00:02:11.557
more than necessary.

24
00:02:11.637 --> 00:02:18.530
It should support query, timeline
reconstruction, alerting, access review,

25
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.116
and evidence preservation.

26
00:02:21.196 --> 00:02:26.209
It should also account for retention and
access control.

27
00:02:26.289 --> 00:02:31.198
Agent observability is not only an
engineering detail;

28
00:02:31.278 --> 00:02:36.500
it is part of the agent's safety and
accountability design.
